  <data name="Efi_With_Safari_Detail" xml:space="preserve"><value>Turns OpenCore's Apple Secure Boot off in the same change: no extra restart later.</value></data>
  <data name="Efi_Safari" xml:space="preserve"><value>Safari on the GPU (optional)</value></data>
  <data name="Efi_Safari_Caption" xml:space="preserve"><value>Misc &gt; Security &gt; SecureBootModel = Disabled, for the system-volume copy of the Metal driver.</value></data>
  <data name="Efi_Safari_Detail" xml:space="preserve"><value>Safari draws on the GPU only with a second copy of the Metal driver on the system volume (system-volume/ssv-install.sh on macOS). Apple Secure Boot refuses to boot such a system volume, so it has to be off. It is a macOS security feature; leave this off if you do not want Safari on the GPU.</value></data>
  <data name="Efi_Acpi_Box" xml:space="preserve"><value>Path for SSDT-ARCOFF.aml</value></data>
  <data name="Efi_Acpi_Confirm" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add SSDT-ARCOFF.aml for this path. macOS is installed, install.sh --apply has run (stage 3), and I checked the path in Device Manager.</value></data>
  <data name="Efi_Acpi_Hint" xml:space="preserve"><value>The shipped table is for {0}; for another path it is adapted to that one. Check yours: Device Manager &gt; Display adapters &gt; the B580 &gt; Properties &gt; Details &gt; Location paths; you can paste the ACPI(...) line. Leave it unticked before macOS is installed: the table hides the B580 from the display driver the installer uses.</value></data>
